ay":0.1092},{"week":16,"opponent":"JAX","home":true,"pf":7.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":43,"epa_per_play":-0.3789,"pass_rate":0.5814,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5676,"def_epa_per_play":-0.03217},{"week":17,"opponent":"BUF","home":false,"pf":17.0,"pa":42.0,"off_snaps":61,"epa_per_play":-0.3758,"pass_rate":0.5902,"pass_rate_neutral":0.625,"def_epa_per_play":0.2551}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"CHI","distance":0.4662,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"DAL","distance":0.4889,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2023,"team":"IND","distance":0.4953,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2019,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.4998,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5103,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"MIN","distance":0.6069,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"SEA","distance":0.6527,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":0.6658,"shared":18,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"},{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.6951,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"DET","distance":0.7227,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Aaron Glenn"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Dolphins went 7-9 in 2018 and were outscored 433 to 319. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 28th in EPA per play and the defense 24th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the 3rd highest no-huddle rate and the 2nd highest base defense usage in the league.","offense":"The Dolphins offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd highest no-huddle rate and the 3rd most receivers on the field in the league. Ryan Tannehill ran the passing game, with Danny Amendola leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Dolphins defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 31st. Robert Quinn led the team with 6.5 sacks, and Cameron Wake added 6 despite playing only 54% of the snaps. Schematically, they had the 2nd highest base defense usage in the league.","special":"The Dolphins were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 13% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 90% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 17% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 39% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Ryan Tannehill played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,979 yards. Targets were spread around, with Danny Amendola and Kenyan Drake leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Frank Gore and Kenyan Drake. On defense, Robert Quinn led the team with 6.5 sacks and Xavien Howard picked off 7 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Bears and the 2020 Cowboys under Kellen Moore. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Vikings and the 2020 Seahawks.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Dolphins changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}